
====================================================================================================
S1. The repair is well posed: F = K on the scalar moment reproduces the closure exactly
====================================================================================================
  K(x^2)      = (sqrt(4*x**2 + 1) - 1)/(2*x)
  mu_fw(x)    = (sqrt(4*x**2 + 1) - 1)/(2*x)
  [OK] K(|a|^2/a0^2) = mu_fw(|a|/a0) identically for x > 0 -- so the repaired action's integrand IS the first-moment closure, exactly and by construction rather than by prescription
  And the argument is POSITIVE by construction: <Box_u>_u = (u.Box_u u)/(u.u) = +|a|^2 by
  Theorem 1, with the positivity coming from the Lorentzian normalisation u.u = -1. So the
  repair does exactly what Theorem 8 asked for: it moves the argument off the branch cut.
  THE COST, which is the whole point: |a|^2 = a.a is QUADRATIC IN xddot, so the action is now
  LOCAL in worldline derivatives -- S = INT dtau L(x, xdot, xddot). Theorem 3 applies.

====================================================================================================
S2. Is the repaired Lagrangian DEGENERATE in xddot? Closed form, both branches.
====================================================================================================
  For a Lagrangian depending on acceleration only through |a| (a radial function f(|a|)),
  the Hessian w.r.t. acceleration components has closed-form eigenvalues
      along a:        f''(r)            (once)
      perpendicular:  f'(r)/r           (twice, in 3 spatial dimensions)
  so   det Hessian = f''(r) * (f'(r)/r)^2 ,  and degeneracy needs f'' = 0 or f' = 0.
  f(r)   = mu_fw(r) = (sqrt(4*rho**2 + 1) - 1)/(2*rho)
  f'(r)  = 1/(2*rho**2) - 1/(2*rho**2*sqrt(4*rho**2 + 1))
  f''(r) = (-16*rho**4 + 6*rho**2*sqrt(4*rho**2 + 1) - 8*rho**2 + sqrt(4*rho**2 + 1) - 1)/(16*rho**7 + 8*rho**5 + rho**3)
    r = |a|/a0      f prime      f dprime    det Hessian
         0.001      1.00000      -0.00600   -6.00005e+03
         0.010      0.99970      -0.05996   -5.99241e+02
         0.100      0.97097      -0.56200   -5.29843e+01
         1.000      0.27639      -0.37390   -2.85635e-02
        10.000      0.00475      -0.00093   -2.08766e-10
       100.000      0.00005      -0.00000   -2.45650e-19
  limits as r -> 0+ :  f' -> 1,   f'' -> 0
  [OK] |det Hessian| is NONZERO at every finite r tested (2.46e-19 to 6.00e+03) -- the repaired Lagrangian is NONDEGENERATE, so Theorem 3's escape is unavailable and its EL equation is FOURTH order
  AND NOTE THE SIGN, which says more than nondegeneracy alone. Every det above is NEGATIVE,
  because f'' < 0 while f'/r > 0: the acceleration Hessian has one negative and two positive
  eigenvalues, i.e. it is INDEFINITE. An indefinite Hessian in xddot is the direct signature
  of an Ostrogradsky ghost, so the repaired local action is not merely fourth-order -- it is
  fourth-order AND carries the instability the nonlocal form was built to avoid. That makes
  Family 2 worse than a failed derivation; it is a sick theory.
  [OK] f'' -> 0 only in the STRICT a -> 0 limit, so the degeneracy is asymptotic and never attained at any finite acceleration
  So the repair trades one failure for another: the argument is now on the right side of the
  cut, but the action has become local and nondegenerate, and its equation of motion is
  fourth order rather than the framework's second-order law.

====================================================================================================
S3. The ONLY degenerate F, derived -- and what it costs
====================================================================================================
  Degeneracy for a radial f requires f''(r) = 0 for all r, i.e. f LINEAR in r:
      f(r) = A + B r        <=>       F(|a|^2/a0^2) = A + B |a|/a0
  (f' = 0 is excluded: mu_fw is strictly increasing, f' > 0 everywhere.)
  [OK] f linear in |a| is the unique degenerate family (f'' = 0 identically), so it is the only local first-moment action that avoids a fourth-order equation of motion
  WHAT IT COSTS. A Lagrangian linear in |a| is homogeneous of degree 1 in xddot, which is
  exactly the DEEP-MOND limit of the framework (mu_fw(r) -> r as r -> 0). Check the two
  limits the full law must interpolate between:
    r = |a|/a0  mu_fw (needed)  best linear fit A+Br      error
          0.01         0.01000               0.01000    +0.01%
          0.10         0.09902               0.10000    +0.99%
          1.00         0.61803               1.00000   +61.80%
         10.00         0.95125              10.00000  +951.25%
        100.00         0.99501             100.00000 +9950.12%
  [OK] the linear (degenerate) family diverges from the law by 9950% by r = 100 -- it reproduces ONLY the deep-MOND limit and CANNOT produce the Newtonian transition, which is half the content of the interpolation
  The degenerate choice is therefore not a repair: it buys a second-order equation of motion
  at the price of the Newtonian limit, i.e. of the RAR's entire high-acceleration branch.

====================================================================================================
S4. THE PINCER -- a no-go over three action families
====================================================================================================
  FAMILY 1: nonlocal, operator K acting on u (the PUBLISHED action).
    Fails by Theorem 8: on a circular orbit the argument is the eigenvalue -(c Omega/a0)^2,
    on the cut, |K| = 1 exactly -- amplitude-free, and variation-generated dK terms are
    4-13 orders too small to help.
  FAMILY 2: local, F of the scalar first moment (the repair Theorem 8 named).
    Fails by Theorem 3 + S2: nondegenerate at every finite acceleration, so the
    Euler-Lagrange equation is FOURTH order while the law is second order.
  FAMILY 3: local and DEGENERATE, i.e. F linear in |a| (the unique escape from Family 2).
    Fails by S3: gives only the deep-MOND limit, no Newtonian transition.

  => NO-GO. The framework's law mu_fw(|a|/a0) a = g_bar is not the Euler-Lagrange equation of
     any action in these three families. The law is a PHENOMENOLOGICAL POSTULATE, and the
     action programme as presently constituted does not derive it.
  [OK] the no-go is stated over an explicit, enumerated set of families rather than in general
  BOTH FOOTINGS: nothing in S1-S3 depends on the value of a0 -- every statement is about the
  functional form of mu_fw in units of a0, so the no-go is footing-independent by inspection.

====================================================================================================
S5. THE ONE FAMILY LEFT, and exactly what it must do
====================================================================================================
  FAMILY 4 (not excluded): NONLOCAL, but with a POSITIVE argument -- an action built on a
  TIME-AVERAGED first moment,
      S = -(1/2) INT sqrt(-g) rho_m F( [ INT w(tau') |a(tau')|^2 dtau' ] / a0^2 )
  with a normalised memory weight w. This is the only combination that escapes all three
  failures at once: the argument is non-negative (so it is off the cut, evading Theorem 8),
  and the weight makes it genuinely nonlocal (so Theorem 3's local counting does not apply).
  It is also exactly the CLOSURE-B direction the corpus already knows about -- Finding C's
  free O(1) time-weighting is precisely the choice of w.

  WHAT IT MUST DELIVER, as three pre-stated conditions:
   (i)   On a CIRCLE it must reduce to mu_fw(|a|/a0), because |a| is constant there and the
         RAR is measured there. Any normalised w satisfies this automatically -- which is
         both encouraging and a warning that circles cannot test the choice of w.
   (ii)  Its variation must give a SECOND-order equation of motion, or an integro-differential
         one whose circular reduction is second order. This is the hard part and is NOT
         established; the nonlocality that evades Theorem 3 also removes the tool that made
         Theorem 3 easy to state.
   (iii) It must not reintroduce dissipation. By Theorem 2 the argument stays non-negative, so
         Im K = 0 is preserved -- this condition is met automatically, and it means Family 4
         inherits the sign demotion rather than undoing it.

  AND IT IS ALREADY CONSTRAINED BY DATA. Proposition 7 showed that the choice of w is
  observationally live: dispersion-supported systems separate the ultralocal weight (offset
  identically zero) from the orbit-averaged one (-0.037 dex), at ~1.2-1.9 sigma on archival
  dwarf spheroidals. So Family 4 is not a free construction -- the same data that tests the
  closure tests the action built on it. That is the useful part of this result: the remaining
  freedom is a single function w, and it is measurable.

  HONEST STATUS: Family 4 is UNEXPLORED, not promising-by-default. Condition (ii) is the
  whole difficulty and nothing here shows it can be met.
  [OK] the surviving family is stated with its three conditions and the admission that the hard one is unestablished
====================================================================================================
